Hyprland, and the Omarchy that rides on it
==========================================

Omarchy is Hyprland. That is why this patch is short: everything Omarchy does, it does through the
compositor, and the compositor answers every question the platform layer asks.

    hyprctl -j monitors        the layout, including scale and which output is focused
    hyprctl -j cursorpos       the global cursor position
    hyprctl -j clients         the window list
    hyprctl -j activewindow    what has focus

That second one is the interesting one. `cursor_position()` returns None on Wayland, and an earlier
patch documents why that is correct rather than lazy: XWayland will answer X queries and hand back a
plausible wrong number, which is worse than no answer. Hyprland is the exception. It publishes the
compositor's own cursor position, so the honest None becomes a real reading.

Finding the compositor is not as simple as it looks
------------------------------------------------Keybindings
-----------
`list_keybindings()` dispatched KDE -> GNOME -> else-XFCE, so on Hyprland it fell through to the XFCE
reader and returned nothing: an empty shortcut list on a desktop with 88 declared bindings.

Two sources, because they answer different questions. `hyprctl -j binds` is what the compositor is
ACTUALLY holding, including runtime bindings, and on plain Hyprland it carries real dispatchers. But
on Omarchy 4.x it returns 223 bindings with the dispatcher `__lua` and a numeric argument on every
single one — structurally useless as a shortcut list. The Lua modules at
/usr/share/omarchy/default/hypr/bindings/*.lua carry the description, so both are read and
de-duplicated.

Measured on the real machine, over SSH with no session environment:

    total: 151    hyprland: 151
    alt+print          Screenrecording
    alt+shift+super+a  Grok                 https://grok.com
    lookup "terminal" -> alt+super+return = terminal-tmux

A stale runtime directory is not a running compositor
-----------------------------------------------------
Scanning $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/hypr/ is right, but a directory alone proves nothing: an exited compositor
leaves its directory behind. Measured, a dead instance keeps `hyprland.log` and `.socket2.sock` while
a live one also has `.socket.sock` (the command socket hyprctl talks to) and `hyprland.lock`. Two
leftover directories from a nested test made a KDE session report itself as Hyprland, which the
capability test caught. The scan now requires `.socket.sock`.

references/OMARCHY-KEYBINDINGS.md ships the harvested set so the defaults are documented rather than
having to be re-derived from a live install.

---
The obvious test is `HYPRLAND_INSTANCE_SIGNATURE`, and it is wrong on its own. That variable is
exported to the processes the compositor *launches*, so a GUI app started from the session has it —
but an SSH shell, a cron job, or anything started from outside the session does not. Measured on
Omarchy 4.0.1 / Hyprland 0.56.2: the variable is absent from **Hyprland's own environment**, so a
check that only reads it reports "no Hyprland" on a machine running Hyprland at that moment.

This was not caught by reading the code. It was caught by connecting to a real Omarchy box and
watching the backend decline to fire.

Resolution order, the same two sources `hyprctl` itself uses:

    1. HYPRLAND_INSTANCE_SIGNATURE if set and non-empty
    2. newest directory under $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/hypr/   <- the compositor writes its signature there
    3. XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP containing "hyprland"       <- weakest, kept only as a last resort

`hyprctl_json()` then passes the resolved signature to hyprctl explicitly, so the result is identical
whether the call comes from a session app, an SSH shell, or cron.

Verified in the worst case, over SSH with nothing set:

    HYPRLAND_INSTANCE_SIGNATURE  (unset)
    XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP          (unset)
    WAYLAND_DISPLAY              (unset)

    session_type         wayland (hyprland, omarchy)
    cursor_position      Some(1447, 226)          matches hyprctl -j cursorpos exactly
    displays()           eDP-2  2560x1600 @ 1.6x  matches hyprctl -j monitors

Omarchy is reported separately
------------------------------
`session_type` says `wayland (hyprland, omarchy)`, not plain `wayland` or plain `hyprland`. Omarchy
4.x replaced the bar and launcher with Quickshell, so the two are different stacks around the same
compositor, and a bug report that cannot tell them apart sends whoever reads it to the wrong place.

The capture chain had the same environment-trust defect: a Hyprland client running nested inside KDE
inherits XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP=KDE and selected kwin-screenshot2, which would have captured the HOST
desktop instead of the compositor the process belongs to. `kwin_screenshot2_available()` now yields
when a Hyprland instance is running.

Verified against a real installation
------------------------------------
Omarchy 4.0.1 packages / 4.0.4 ISO, 1146 packages: hyprland 0.56.2, quickshell 0.3.1,
hyprtoolkit 0.5.4, xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland 1.4.1, /usr/bin/omarchy and /usr/bin/hyprctl present.
No waybar, no walker, no mako — any support written against the waybar-era stack is wrong here.

`examples/platform_probe` ships so the same check can be run on any compositor; `ci/hypr-nested.sh`
starts a throwaway nested Hyprland to develop against without switching sessions.

